     To look at and change the contents of the memory of the system you are
     running on,

           gdb -k -wcore kernel.debug /dev/mem

     In this mode, you need the --kk flag to indicate to gdb(1)
     (_p_o_r_t_s_/_d_e_v_e_l_/_g_d_b) that the “dump file” _/_d_e_v_/_m_e_m is a kernel data file.
     You can look at live data, and if you include the --wwccoorree option, you can
     change it at your peril.  The system does not stop (obviously), so a num‐
     ber of things will not work.  You can set breakpoints, but you cannot
     “continue” execution, so they will not work.

